{
 "turns": [
  {
   "idx": 0,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Grace Oileron, and this must be the patient. Nice shin guards, buddy.",
   "start": 0.08,
   "end": 8.28
  },
  {
   "idx": 1,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "Game today. Right after this, so he's in a hurry, I'm his dad Javier.",
   "start": 9.39,
   "end": 14.35
  },
  {
   "idx": 2,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Nice to meet you. Can you confirm his full name and date of birth for me?",
   "start": 15.11,
   "end": 21.02
  },
  {
   "idx": 3,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "Mateo Herrera, April 21st, 2017. He's nine.",
   "start": 21.68,
   "end": 26.22
  },
  {
   "idx": 4,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Thanks, so what are we looking at today?",
   "start": 27.13,
   "end": 29.75
  },
  {
   "idx": 5,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "Warts, he's got a couple on his right pointer finger, one on his left thumb, and one on his knee. The finger ones started maybe five months ago, and they keep multiplying.",
   "start": 29.75,
   "end": 40.49
  },
  {
   "idx": 6,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Have you tried anything for them?",
   "start": 41.29,
   "end": 43.05
  },
  {
   "idx": 7,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "The drugstore salicylic acid, the 17 % liquid, every night for about six weeks, didn't really do much.",
   "start": 43.55,
   "end": 51.11
  },
  {
   "idx": 8,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "That's pretty common. It can take months. Do they hurt him?",
   "start": 51.57,
   "end": 56.56
  },
  {
   "idx": 9,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "No, not really. He picks at them, though, and bites his nails.",
   "start": 56.59,
   "end": 60.71
  },
  {
   "idx": 10,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Have they bled or gotten infected?",
   "start": 61.53,
   "end": 63.89
  },
  {
   "idx": 11,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "No.",
   "start": 65.12,
   "end": 65.46
  },
  {
   "idx": 12,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Is he generally healthy? Any problems with his immune system or a lot of infections?",
   "start": 66.14,
   "end": 73.8
  },
  {
   "idx": 13,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "No, he's healthy. No medicines, no allergies. Oh, and his sister Lucia has one on the bottom of her foot, if that matters. She's not here today. Good to know they do spread in families.",
   "start": 74.79,
   "end": 87.19
  },
  {
   "idx": 14,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Okay Mateo, let me see your hands. On the right index finger, there are two rough, skin -colored bumps, 5mm and 3mm, with tiny black dots in them. On the left thumb, there's a 6mm one right next to the nail fold. And on the right knee, a 4mm one. These are all common warts, no redness or signs of infection around them.",
   "start": 88.62,
   "end": 119.34
  },
  {
   "idx": 15,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "Can you also look at his arms? He has these little rough bumps on the backs of his upper arms, like sandpaper.",
   "start": 119.78,
   "end": 126.04
  },
  {
   "idx": 16,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Sure, yes, on the backs of both upper arms. There are lots of tiny, pinpoint, rough bumps around the hair follicles. Skin colored to pink, that's keratosis pilaris. It's really common, it's harmless, and it often gets better as kids get older.",
   "start": 127.08,
   "end": 150.44
  },
  {
   "idx": 17,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "Oh good, is there anything for it?",
   "start": 151.21,
   "end": 153.43
  },
  {
   "idx": 18,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "A urea 10 % cream once a day after the bath helps smooth it out. It's over the counter. Use a gentle soap, and don't scrub hard. Now for the warts. I'd like to freeze them today with liquid nitrogen. It stings for a few seconds. Is that okay, Mateo?",
   "start": 153.77,
   "end": 178.68
  },
  {
   "idx": 19,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "He's nodding. Go for it.",
   "start": 179.36,
   "end": 181.14
  },
  {
   "idx": 20,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "All right. I'm doing all four warts. Two freestyle cycles each. Here comes the cold. Great job. You didn't even flinch.",
   "start": 181.92,
   "end": 192.91
  },
  {
   "idx": 21,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "What should we expect now?",
   "start": 195.04,
   "end": 196.34
  },
  {
   "idx": 22,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "They may get red, form a blister, or turn dark over the next few days. That's normal. Children's acetaminophen per the package directions, if they are sore. Once the blisters heal, in about a week, restart the salicylic acid at night. Soak first, file the dead skin with an emery board that only he uses, then cover duct tape.",
   "start": 197.3,
   "end": 228.1
  },
  {
   "idx": 23,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "Duct tape, really?",
   "start": 228.56,
   "end": 229.62
  },
  {
   "idx": 24,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Really, it helps keep the medicine on, and try to keep him from picking and biting. That's how they spread. If one gets very red, swollen, or his pus, give us a call.",
   "start": 230.32,
   "end": 245.72
  },
  {
   "idx": 25,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "Got it. Does he need to stay home from school? No, he can go to school and play sports. Where does he go? Maple Grove Elementary, 4th grade.",
   "start": 246.22,
   "end": 257.55
  },
  {
   "idx": 26,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Nice. Warts usually take two to four freezing sessions, so let's bring him back in three to four weeks for another round. What's the best number for you?\"",
   "start": 258.45,
   "end": 271.4
  },
  {
   "idx": 27,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "Mycel 555 -0191.",
   "start": 271.4,
   "end": 274.58
  },
  {
   "idx": 28,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Perfect.",
   "start": 275.36,
   "end": 275.96
  },
  {
   "idx": 29,
   "speaker": "caregiver",
   "text": "Good luck at the game, Mateo. Thanks, Dr. Oyelaren. Say thank you, Mateo. Okay, he said it.",
   "start": 277.0,
   "end": 283.98
  }
 ],
